-- A recursive definition runs its body many times per call, so the
-- realizer prices a call of it as its body's cost times 1000 (COST-14).
-- `count` is defined with operators available in Python and C++, and its
-- caller hands the result to a Python function. Priced once, the body
-- would stay in Python beside its caller; priced per level, it runs in
-- C++, which costs one crossing into C++ and back. The placement listing
-- of `morloc typecheck --realize` is the output; the run checks the
-- program still computes.

--' Recursion priced per level
module main (countFrom)

import root-py
import root-cpp

--' Count down to zero
count :: Int -> Int
count n
  ? n == 0 = 0
  : 1 + count (n - 1)

--' Count down to zero and pass the count through Python
countFrom :: Int -> Int
countFrom n = idpy (count n)
